bandpass filter.py
numpy and scipy
Imports
dataclass and Iterable: used for a small config structure and to accept any list‑like signal input.
numpy: turns the signal into a clean numeric array and handles NaNs.
scipy.signal functions:
butter: designs the Butterworth filter.
sosfiltfilt: applies the filter in forward+reverse (zero‑phase).
iirnotch + filtfilt: makes and applies the 60 Hz notch.
BandpassFilterConfig
A small immutable config holder for filter settings. It’s optional but useful if you want to pass settings as a single object later.

BandpassFilter.design_butterworth(...)
Converts the low/high cut into normalized frequencies based on Nyquist (half the sampling rate).
Returns filter coefficients in SOS (second‑order sections), which are numerically stable.
BandpassFilter.apply(...)
This is the main entry point.

Convert input to a float array.
If empty, return immediately.
Replace NaN values with interpolated values so filtering doesn’t break.
Design Butterworth bandpass and apply sosfiltfilt (zero‑phase filtering).
If notch_freq is valid (not above Nyquist), apply a 60 Hz notch.
Return the filtered signal.
apply_ecg(...)
Convenience wrapper for ECG defaults:

0.5–40 Hz bandpass at 250 Hz.
apply_ppg(...)
Convenience wrapper for PPG defaults:

0.5–8 Hz bandpass at 125 Hz.
_safe_sosfiltfilt(...) and _safe_filtfilt(...)
filtfilt requires the signal length to be longer than a padding length. If it’s too short, we reflect‑pad the signal first so filtering still works.

_reflect_pad(...)
Reflect‑pads the signal to satisfy the minimum length requirement.

_fill_nans(...)
If NaNs exist:

If all values are NaN, return zeros.
Otherwise, linearly interpolate missing values.
Why this matches your spec
Bandpass + notch: exactly as specified.
Zero‑phase filtering: uses sosfiltfilt.
Edge cases: handles short signals and NaNs.
ECG/PPG configs: hardcoded helpers for the exact ranges you gave.

File: backend/app/services/ingestion.py
Imports
dataclass, Optional
Used to define a simple data container and allow optional fields like patient_id.
BytesIO
Lets pandas read a CSV from raw bytes (file upload).
numpy as np
Used for numeric arrays and timestamp generation.
pandas as pd
Used to parse CSV files and fill missing values.
wfdb
Reads MIT‑BIH and MIMIC waveform records (rdrecord, rdann).
get_settings
Loads dataset paths from your config.
@dataclass RawSignalData
A small, immutable object that represents any raw signal input in a consistent way.

Fields:

signal_type: string like "ECG" or "PPG".
source: where it came from (MIT_BIH_AF, MIMIC_PPG, SIMULATED, etc.).
values: numpy array of signal values.
sampling_rate: numeric sampling frequency.
timestamps: array of timestamps aligned to the samples.
patient_id, session_id: optional identifiers.
labels: optional list of labels (like "AFIB").
class IngestionService
This class groups all the ways you ingest signals.

__init__
Loads settings once and stores them in _settings.

load_mitbih(record_id, lead=0)
Reads MIT‑BIH AFDB data.

Steps:

Build path from settings.afdb_dir / record_id.
Read waveform using wfdb.rdrecord.
Pick a lead (column) from p_signal.
Build timestamps from index / sampling rate.
Try to read annotations (rdann).
Normalize any label "AFIB" to "AFIB" (kept consistent).
Return a RawSignalData object.
load_mimic(record_id, lead=0)
Same logic as MIT‑BIH, but uses dataset root path:
datasets_root / cardiac/mimic_perform_af.

It’s still waveform + annotations, returned as RawSignalData.

load_csv(file_bytes, signal_type, sample_rate, column)
Reads uploaded CSVs.

Steps:

Read CSV from bytes with pandas.
Pick a column (by index or name).
Force numeric type.
Interpolate missing values linearly.
Convert to numpy array.
Build timestamps from sample rate.
Return RawSignalData.
load_simulated(signal_type, duration_seconds, scenario)
Generates synthetic data.

Steps:

Decide sampling rate: 250 Hz for ECG, 125 Hz for PPG.
Build time array.
If scenario is "afib":
Add random phase jitter to simulate irregular rhythm.
Else: use clean sinus rhythm.
Add small noise.
Return RawSignalData with labels like ["AFIB"] or ["SINUS"].
