Short answer: it will train, but “accuracy” isn’t guaranteed, because the data pipeline is minimal and the labels are coarse.

Concrete reasons:

Labels are weak: MIT‑BIH labels in this script are taken from annotations, but we map any non‑AFIB label to 0. That loses granularity and can mislabel segments.
Very small dataset: only four records are used. That’s not enough for a reliable CNN.
Segment quality filter: windows below quality 0.4 are dropped; you might end up with very few training samples.
PPG transfer: uses the same small record list and no true PPG labels.
So the model will train and output metrics, but you should expect unstable or low accuracy unless you expand the dataset and improve labeling.

If you want it to train for real performance, I can:

Load more records from AFDB and MIMIC.
Build label arrays aligned to annotations per sample.
Stratify by record, not just by segment, to avoid leakage.





















































































